  <section id="start">
    <h2><span class="n">1</span>Install &amp; first sound</h2>
    <p class="lead">Copy the bundle into your plugin folder, rescan, add it to an
      instrument track. No installer, no activation server, no internet check.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>OS</th><th>VST3</th><th>CLAP</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Windows</td><td class="r">C:\Program Files\Common Files\VST3\</td><td class="r">C:\Program Files\Common Files\CLAP\</td></tr>
          <tr><td>macOS</td><td class="r">~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3/</td><td class="r">~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/CLAP/</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux</td><td class="r">~/.vst3/</td><td class="r">~/.clap/</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>VST3 is a folder, CLAP is a single file; they sound identical — pick
      whichever your DAW prefers. Then:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Arm / input-monitor the track</b> or a MIDI keyboard never reaches
        the plugin. In REAPER that's the <kbd>R</kbd> button on the track.</li>
      <li>Play. It opens clean: <span class="set">Drive/Fold/Grit/FM/Feedback = 0</span>,
        <span class="set">Filter = Off</span>, <span class="set">Brightness = 35%</span> —
        a near-sine with a gentle rolloff.</li>
      <li>Turn <b>Brightness</b> up for more harmonics; step the preset arrows
        <span class="set">◀ name ▶</span> at the top to hear the 22 starter patches.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout amber">
      <span class="lbl">Loads but silent</span>
      Almost always the track isn't monitoring. After that: default <b>Gain</b> is
      −12&nbsp;dB and <b>Brightness</b> 35&nbsp;% — raise both; make sure
      <b>Filter</b> is Off or its cutoff is high.
    </div>
    <div class="callout teal">
      <span class="lbl">Registering Studio</span>
      When you buy a licence you get a small text key file with your name on it.
      Drop it at <span class="set">%APPDATA%\Cosine\license.key</span> (Windows),
      <span class="set">~/Library/Application&nbsp;Support/Cosine/license.key</span> (macOS)
      or <span class="set">~/.config/Cosine/license.key</span> (Linux) and reopen
      the plugin — the title bar then shows your name and the Studio controls
      light up. It's checked once, offline; nothing is sent anywhere, and the
      same file works on all your machines.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="osc">
    <h2><span class="n">2</span>The oscillator: Brightness, Partials, Formant</h2>
    <p class="lead">Pick the <b>Oscillator</b> first. <em>Geometric</em> is the
      closed-form additive core and the only one that responds to Brightness,
      Partials, Formant, Expr and HQ. <em>Saw</em> and <em>Triangle</em> are
      band-limited PolyBLEP / PolyBLAMP with a fixed <code>1/k</code> /
      <code>1/k²</code> spectrum — flat all the way down to DC, and they ignore
      Brightness and HQ.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Oscillator</td><td class="r">Geometric / Saw / Triangle</td><td>Geometric = the additive core. Saw / Triangle = classic band-limited waves, fixed spectrum.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Brightness</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Spectral <em>tilt</em>. The knob is <code>r</code>; harmonic <em>k</em> is scaled by <code>rᵏ</code>. 0 % ≈ pure sine, 100 % ≈ bright impulse. Geometric only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Partials</td><td class="r">1–2048</td><td>A hard <em>ceiling</em> on the harmonic count — a smooth sweep from sine (1) to full impulse (2048), fractional values fade the next harmonic in. <b>Costs the same at every value and never aliases.</b> Geometric only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Formant <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>A movable resonant bump in the mid overtones (a vowel-ish colour). Low values put the bump low (≈ 2nd harmonic), high values high (≈ 26th) and deepen it. A <em>second</em> closed-form term — free. <code>0 %</code> = off. Geometric only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Expr → Bright <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">−100…+100 %</td><td>Per-note brightness from MPE / aftertouch — see <a href="#mod">§7</a>. Default <code>+35 %</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Gain</td><td class="r">−60…0 dB</td><td>Master level, before the soft clipper.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Brightness vs Partials — two different tools</h3>
    <p><b>Brightness</b> tilts: every harmonic stays present, the highs just get
      quieter. <b>Partials</b> cuts: harmonics above the ceiling are simply gone.
      Use Brightness for tone colour, Partials for a filter-like sweep that costs
      nothing and can't alias — automate it, put an LFO nowhere near it and it
      still holds. They stack: a bright tilt with a low ceiling is a resonant-ish
      pluck. Both are in <b>Core</b>.</p>

    <div class="callout teal">
      <span class="lbl">Why "free"</span>
      The whole tone is one algebraic expression — a truncated complex geometric
      series (Lagrange's identity, ~1830s). Rendering 2000 harmonics costs the
      same as rendering 3: about <b>Θ(log n)</b> per sample, and <b>flat within
      1 %</b> from 3 to 1200 harmonics because the one expensive term is cached on
      a held note. There is no per-partial oscillator loop.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="dirt">
    <h2><span class="n">3</span>Making it dirty: Character, FM, Feedback <span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--amber-ink)">(Studio)</span></h2>
    <p class="lead">The clean core is the right foundation because you can always
      dirty a clean signal and never clean a dirty one. Everything here is
      deliberate grit under a knob — <b>at zero it is bit-clean</b>. This whole
      section is <b>Studio</b>; in Core the chain stays clean.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Drive</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Asymmetric saturation (bias moves with it). Fattening, even harmonics.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Fold</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Reflective wavefolder. Dense, evolving upper spectrum.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Grit</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Bit-crush + sample-rate reduction together. Early-digital character.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">FM Amount</td><td class="r">0–4</td><td>Phase-modulation depth (sine operator).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">FM Ratio</td><td class="r">0.5–12</td><td>Modulator ratio. Integers = harmonic, fractional = clangorous.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Feedback</td><td class="r">0–0.9</td><td>Operator self-feedback. Sine → saw → noise as you push it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Measured brightening (high/low-mid energy ratio, from the engine's own
      demo): clean <code>0.01</code> → Fold <code>0.10</code> → Feedback
      <code>0.31</code> → FM <code>0.78</code>.</p>

    <div class="callout amber">
      <span class="lbl">These stages alias on purpose</span>
      Drive / Fold / Grit / FM / Feedback and the master soft-clip push content
      past Nyquist and <em>will</em> fold it back down when pushed hard — that's
      the PPG / DX7 character. For a clean result turn on <b>HQ Mode</b>
      (<a href="#voice">§5</a>) and watch the aliasing meter
      (<a href="#display">§9</a>) tell you whether you need it.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="filter">
    <h2><span class="n">4</span>Filter &amp; the two envelopes</h2>
    <p class="lead">A zero-delay-feedback state-variable filter (Cytomic), after
      the character stage. It cannot self-oscillate. Cutoff and resonance are
      smoothed per sample — no zipper on automation. The filter itself is in
      <b>Core</b>; the dedicated filter envelope is <b>Studio</b>.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Filter</td><td class="r">Off / LP / BP / HP / Notch</td><td>Mode. <em>Off</em> is a true bypass.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Cutoff</td><td class="r">20 Hz – 20 kHz</td><td>Corner frequency, per-sample smoothed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Resonance</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Q sweeps <code>0.5 → 32</code>. Capped — won't ring out of control.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Filter Env <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">−6…+6 octaves</td><td>A <em>dedicated</em> envelope routed to cutoff, bipolar. Negative = the filter closes on attack.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">F.Env A / D / S / R <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">—</td><td>That envelope's own ADSR, fully independent of the amp envelope.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The amp envelope is AR, the filter envelope is full ADSR</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Attack</td><td class="r">0.5 ms – 2 s</td><td>Linear fade-in.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Release</td><td class="r">5 ms – 5 s</td><td>Exponential fade-out. Sustain is fixed at full — this is an AR envelope.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Envelope times are honest: a <code>t</code>-second decay/release stage
      actually <em>finishes</em> in about <code>t</code>, not <code>5t</code>. On
      the filter envelope, <code>Sustain = 0</code> gives a percussive sweep that
      closes down while the note holds underneath — a plucked-string trick.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="voice">
    <h2><span class="n">5</span>Voices, unison &amp; HQ mode</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Free-Run Phase</td><td class="r">on / off</td><td>Analogue style — the oscillator phase is <em>not</em> reset on note-on, so repeated notes differ slightly.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">HQ Mode <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">on / off</td><td>2× oversample the whole chain so Drive / Fold / Grit / FM don't alias. <b>+16 samples latency</b> (reported to the host; PDC compensates, and it stays constant whether HQ is on or off). CPU barely moves — but it doesn't drop either.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Unison</td><td class="r">1–8</td><td>Stack of detuned, stereo-spread, phase-decorrelated voices per note (<code>1/√n</code> make-up gain). Eats into the 24-voice pool.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Uni Detune</td><td class="r">0–50 ¢</td><td>Spread of the stack in cents.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Uni Spread</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>Stereo width of the stack.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Uni Drift</td><td class="r">0–100 %</td><td>A slow, independent per-voice phase drift that makes the stereo image <em>breathe</em>. This is the "analogue" in the analogue stack.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout teal">
      <span class="lbl">HQ workflow (Studio)</span>
      Don't leave HQ on by reflex. Dial the sound, look at the aliasing meter on
      the right of the spectrum: <span class="swatch s-good"></span>green — HQ
      would only add latency, leave it off. <span class="swatch s-warn"></span>amber
      — borderline. <span class="swatch s-bad"></span>red — the nonlinear stages
      are folding audible aliasing; turn HQ on and the bar drops.
    </div>
    <div class="callout amber">
      <span class="lbl">One known trade-off</span>
      Stealing an <em>actively sounding</em> voice past the 24-voice limit clicks
      — the de-click only fades in, not out. Fast passages beyond polyphony, or
      heavy unison, will tick. Use fewer unison voices or shorter releases.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tuning">
    <h2><span class="n">6</span>Microtuning &amp; Scala files <span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--amber-ink)">(Studio)</span></h2>
    <p class="lead">Because the oscillator builds its overtones <em>on</em>
      <code>k·f0</code> of a retuned fundamental, a chord in just intonation is
      perfectly in tune with itself — the beating stops. The wavetable and
      subtractive worlds can't do that structurally; their oscillator ships with
      fixed overtones. Anything beyond 12-TET / A = 440 is <b>Studio</b>; Core
      plays standard 12-TET.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Tuning</td><td class="r">Equal / Just / Pythagorean / ¼-comma Meantone / 19-EDO / 24-EDO / 31-EDO / Bohlen-Pierce</td><td>Note→frequency map. <em>Equal</em> is the default and is bit-for-bit ordinary 12-TET. The rest retune the <b>fundamental</b>. Takes effect on the next note.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Tune Root</td><td class="r">C … B</td><td>Tonic of the 12-note historical scales (Just / Pythagorean / Meantone). Ignored by Equal and the EDOs.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Tune Ref</td><td class="r">415–467 Hz</td><td>Reference pitch (A4). <code>440</code> standard; <code>432</code> etc. scale the whole tuning.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Scala</td><td class="r">.scl path or pasted text</td><td>Load any Scala scale — ratios (<code>3/2</code>) or cents (<code>701.955</code>), up to 64 notes. Overrides the Tuning dropdown while set. <code>✕</code> clears it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Keymap</td><td class="r">.kbm path or pasted text</td><td>A Scala keyboard map: which physical key plays which scale degree, with dead keys (<code>x</code>) that sound nothing. Maps onto the loaded <code>.scl</code> (or a 12-EDO chromatic if none). While loaded it carries its own reference pitch, so <b>Tune Root / Tune Ref are ignored</b>. <code>✕</code> clears it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Workflow: a 7-note scale on the white keys only</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste a 7-note scale into <b>Scala</b> — e.g. a 5-limit major:
        <span class="set">major<br>7<br>9/8<br>5/4<br>4/3<br>3/2<br>5/3<br>15/8<br>2/1</span>
        (first line is a name, second the note count, then the degrees, last the period).</li>
      <li>Paste a <b>Keymap</b> that maps the 7 white keys of each octave to
        degrees 0–6 and marks the 5 black keys dead. A 12-key <code>.kbm</code>
        pattern: middle note, reference note + frequency, then
        <span class="set">0  x  1  x  2  3  x  4  x  5  x  6</span>.</li>
      <li>Black keys now make no sound; each octave of white keys walks the scale
        and repeats. The <code>.kbm</code>'s own reference pitch anchors it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Both files save with the project. Everything is parsed without allocating
      on the audio thread, so changing tuning mid-session never glitches.</p>

    <div class="cando">
      <div class="yes"><h4>Microtuning does</h4><ul>
        <li>Just intonation, Pythagorean, meantone, any <em>n</em>-EDO, Bohlen-Pierce</li>
        <li>Arbitrary Scala <code>.scl</code> scales (ratios or cents, ≤ 64 notes)</li>
        <li><code>.kbm</code> keyboard maps with dead keys and a per-file anchor</li>
        <li>Any A4 reference (415–467 Hz)</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="no"><h4>It does not</h4><ul>
        <li>Move an individual overtone off <code>k·f0</code> — bell inharmonicity, stretched-piano tuning</li>
        <li>Take a host MTS-ESP feed (not implemented yet)</li>
        <li>Retune notes already sounding — the scale applies to the next note-on</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="mod">
    <h2><span class="n">7</span>Modulation &amp; per-note expression</h2>
    <p class="lead">Bend Range is in <b>Core</b>; the whole LFO matrix and per-note
      expression are <b>Studio</b>.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Bend Range</td><td class="r">1–24 semitones</td><td>Pitch-wheel range.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO Rate <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">0.02–30 Hz</td><td>Per-voice LFO speed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO Shape <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">sine / tri / saw</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO Sync <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">Retrigger / Free-Run</td><td>Retrigger resets phase on note-on; Free-Run runs through notes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO → Bright <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">±100 %</td><td>To the spectral tilt. Shows on the spectrum comb in real time.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO → Vibrato <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">0–100 ¢</td><td>To pitch.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO → Cutoff <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">±4 octaves</td><td>To the filter cutoff.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">LFO → FM <b>(Studio)</b></td><td class="r">±4</td><td>To the FM index.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Any target at <code>0</code> is skipped; if <em>all</em> targets are zero
      the LFO isn't computed at all (and the patch is bit-clean).</p>

    <h3>Per-note brightness (MPE / aftertouch) <span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--amber-ink)">(Studio)</span></h3>
    <p><b>Expr → Bright</b> (<code>−100…+100 %</code>, default <code>+35 %</code>)
      routes three expression sources to the spectral tilt <em>per key</em>: MPE
      timbre (CC 74), poly aftertouch, and channel pressure. Press harder on one
      note of a chord and only <em>that</em> note gets more overtones — the
      expressiveness a wavetable fakes with a filter. Negative inverts it (harder
      → darker). <code>0 %</code> disables it and is bit-identical to no
      expression. Geometric oscillator only. Enable MPE mode in your DAW /
      controller; the plugin is monotimbral but addresses brightness per key.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="work">
    <h2><span class="n">8</span>Workflows: A/B morph, seed codes, presets</h2>

    <h3>A/B morph</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Dial a sound → <b>SET A</b> (snapshots every parameter into slot A).</li>
      <li>Change anything → <b>SET B</b>.</li>
      <li>Drag the slider: <code>0</code> = exactly A, <code>1</code> = exactly B,
        between = a clean blend of <em>every</em> parameter.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The slider writes interpolated values into the real knobs, so the sound
      matches the GUI and your DAW records the move as ordinary automation.
      Discrete switches (Oscillator, Filter type, LFO shape, HQ, Free-Run) snap at
      the halfway point — keep them the same in A and B for a pure timbral morph.
      Slots and position save with the project. Works in Core (it just won't move
      the Studio-only knobs).</p>

    <h3>Seed randomiser</h3>
    <p><b>RANDOM</b> rolls a new patch (press until you like one). The box shows a
      <b>6-character code</b>; send it to anyone and they get the exact same
      sound, on any OS or DAW — the seed→patch mapping is integer arithmetic, like
      the rest of the engine's determinism. Type a code + <kbd>Enter</kbd> to load
      it. RANDOM only touches sound parameters (not HQ / Bend Range / Free-Run)
      and stays in musical ranges, so usable patches come up often.</p>
    <div class="callout amber">
      <span class="lbl">Codes are version-tied</span>
      A code drifts from the sound the moment you touch a knob, and it's coupled
      to the plugin version. For <em>archiving</em> a patch, save the project
      state or an A/B slot — not the code.
    </div>

    <h3>Preset bank</h3>
    <p>22 starter patches, each verified to render bounded, non-silent audio that
      actually releases. Step them with the <span class="set">◀ name ▶</span>
      arrows. They're a starting point, not the product — the point of the synth
      is that few, transparent parameters make your <em>own</em> sound reachable.
      In Core, presets that lean on Studio features simply sound like their Core
      version.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Presets</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bass</td><td>Deep Sub · Reese · FM Bass · Formant Growl</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lead</td><td>Bright Saw Lead · Hollow Fifth · Screamer · Vintage FM Lead</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pad</td><td>Glass Pad · Warm Analog Pad · Choir-ish · Evolving Drone</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Keys / plucked</td><td>Dulcimer Pluck · Electric Piano · Music Box · Clav</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bell / metallic</td><td>DX Bell · Metallic Drop</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Texture</td><td>Digital Grit · Wavefolder Sweep · Noise Wash</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="display">
    <h2><span class="n">9</span>Reading the spectrum display <span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--amber-ink)">(Studio)</span></h2>
    <p class="lead">Not an FFT — it draws the actual maths and a cheap
      measurement side by side. The closed-form overlay and the aliasing meter
      are Studio; Core shows the plain 30-band spectrum.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Green bars</b> — a cheap 30-band picture of what's coming out.</li>
      <li><b>Amber comb</b> — the closed-form partials the current patch defines
        for the note you're playing: <code>rᵏ</code> from Brightness plus the
        Formant hump, cut at the Partials ceiling (the vertical amber line). The
        tilt is <b>live</b> — LFO → Bright breathes it. With unison on, each
        partial widens into a band of the same pixel width — that's why unison
        thickens the tone.</li>
      <li><b>Teal curve</b> — the filter response for the current Cutoff /
        Resonance, with a mark at the cutoff, drawn whenever Filter isn't Off. It
        follows the <b>live</b> cutoff, so you watch the filter envelope and
        LFO → Cutoff sweep it; a faint tick shows where the knob rests.</li>
      <li><b>Hover</b> Brightness, Partials, Formant, Cutoff or Resonance — the
        display adds faint "ghost" curves for a few settings of that control, a
        look at what the knob does before you touch it.</li>
      <li><b>Coloured bar, right edge</b> — the honest aliasing meter, energy just
        below Nyquist. <span class="swatch s-good"></span>&lt; −45 dBFS clean ·
        <span class="swatch s-warn"></span>borderline ·
        <span class="swatch s-bad"></span>&gt; −30 dBFS the nonlinear stages are
        folding audible aliasing → turn on HQ.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The clean oscillator puts nothing up near Nyquist, so when the meter lights
      it is the character / FM / feedback stages — unless Brightness is very high,
      which puts real high harmonics in that band (if every nonlinear knob is at
      zero, it isn't aliasing and HQ won't help).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="keys">
    <h2><span class="n">10</span>Editor shortcuts</h2>
    <p class="lead">The editor is a <b>468 × 760</b> scrolling panel with eight
      grouped sections: TONE, AMP ENVELOPE, CHARACTER, FM, FILTER, VOICE, TUNING,
      MODULATION. Studio-only rows are greyed in Core. Every slider takes the same
      modifiers:</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Gesture</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><kbd>Alt</kbd> + click</td><td>Type an exact value.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>⌘</kbd> + click &nbsp;·&nbsp; double-click &nbsp;·&nbsp; right-click</td><td>Reset the parameter to its default.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd> + drag</td><td>Fine adjustment (10× slower).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Drag</td><td>Normal adjustment.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scroll wheel</td><td>Deliberately disabled on the sliders — so scrolling the panel never nudges a value.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hover a Tone / Filter slider</td><td>Ghost curves in the spectrum (<a href="#display">§9</a>).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Everything else — bypass, undo, A/B plugin-level compare, parameter
      automation lanes — is your host's, and works because every move the editor
      makes goes through the host as an ordinary automatable parameter change.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="midi">
    <h2><span class="n">11</span>MIDI reference</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Handling</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="p">Note On / Off</td><td>Voice allocation, unison stacking. 24 voices (unison shares the pool). Under a <code>.kbm</code> map, dead keys are ignored.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Choke</td><td>Instant mute — ignores the sustain pedal.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Pitch Bend</td><td>All sounding voices, smoothed. Range = <b>Bend Range</b>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">CC 64 — sustain pedal</td><td>While held (<code>value ≥ 0.5</code>) note-offs are deferred; on release all held notes let go.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">CC 123 — all notes off</td><td>Frees every voice, resets the pedal.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Poly Aftertouch</td><td>Per-note brightness for that key (Studio). Depth = <b>Expr → Bright</b>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">MPE timbre — CC 74</td><td>Per-note brightness, the MPE Y-axis (Studio). Depth = <b>Expr → Bright</b>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p">Channel Pressure</td><td>Brightness shared across all sounding notes (Studio). Depth = <b>Expr → Bright</b>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The plugin declares <code>MidiConfig::MidiCCs</code>, so pitch bend, the
      sustain pedal and CC 123 need your DAW to pass raw MIDI CC through — some
      hosts have a per-track "pass MIDI CC" switch. It's monotimbral (no split by
      MIDI channel), <b>but per-note brightness is addressed per key</b>, so MPE
      expression works.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="recipes">
    <h2><span class="n">12</span>Patch recipes</h2>
    <p class="lead">Starting points, in real parameter values. Load
      <em>Init</em> first. Most of these use Studio controls — the labels say which.</p>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>Pad that's actually in tune <span style="color:var(--amber-ink);font-size:.8rem">· Studio (tuning)</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">Just-intonation chord, no beating, breathing stereo.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="set">Tuning = Just Intonation</span>, <span class="set">Tune Root</span> to your song's key.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Brightness 55 %</span>, <span class="set">Partials ≈ 700</span>, <span class="set">Attack 400 ms</span>, <span class="set">Release 1.5 s</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Unison 4</span>, <span class="set">Uni Detune 8 ¢</span>, <span class="set">Uni Spread 60 %</span>, <span class="set">Uni Drift 45 %</span>.</li>
        <li>Optional <span class="set">Filter LP</span>, cutoff ~4 kHz, <span class="set">LFO → Cutoff ±0.5 oct</span> at <span class="set">0.15 Hz</span>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>Formant vowel lead <span style="color:var(--amber-ink);font-size:.8rem">· Studio</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">A talking, resonant mono line.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="set">Oscillator Geometric</span>, <span class="set">Brightness 40 %</span>, <span class="set">Formant 45 %</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="set">LFO Shape sine</span>, <span class="set">Rate 5 Hz</span>, <span class="set">LFO → Bright 25 %</span> — the hump slides.</li>
        <li>Short <span class="set">Attack</span>, moderate <span class="set">Release</span>; <span class="set">Filter BP</span> around the formant for extra focus.</li>
        <li>Play with <b>Expr → Bright</b> on an MPE controller — press for the vowel to open.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>DX-style bell <span style="color:var(--amber-ink);font-size:.8rem">· Studio</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">Inharmonic attack, long shimmering tail.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="set">FM Amount 2.5</span>, <span class="set">FM Ratio 3.5</span> (or <span class="set">7</span> for a cleaner bell), <span class="set">Feedback 0.15</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Brightness 30 %</span> — let FM do the highs.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Filter Env −3 oct</span>, <span class="set">F.Env Decay 300 ms</span>, <span class="set">Sustain 0</span> — the attack is bright, the tail darkens.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Release 3 s</span>. Add tiny <span class="set">Uni Detune 3 ¢</span> for width.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>PPG-grit bass <span style="color:var(--amber-ink);font-size:.8rem">· Studio</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">Dirty digital low end — and knowing when to spend the latency.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Low note. <span class="set">Brightness 45 %</span>, <span class="set">Drive 55 %</span>, <span class="set">Fold 35 %</span>, <span class="set">Grit 40 %</span>.</li>
        <li>Look at the aliasing meter. If it's <span class="swatch s-bad"></span>red and you want it clean, <span class="set">HQ Mode on</span> — the bar drops, you pay 16 samples.</li>
        <li>If it's <span class="swatch s-warn"></span>amber and the part is busy, leave HQ off — you won't hear it.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Filter LP</span> ~2 kHz with a little resonance to tame the top.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>Breathing analogue stack <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem">· Core-friendly</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">Wide, alive, never quite static.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="set">Free-Run Phase on</span> — every note starts differently.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Unison 6</span>, <span class="set">Uni Detune 14 ¢</span>, <span class="set">Uni Spread 70 %</span>, <span class="set">Uni Drift 60 %</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="set">Oscillator Saw</span> for the classic base, or Geometric at <span class="set">Brightness 65 %</span>.</li>
        <li>Slow <span class="set">LFO → Vibrato 4 ¢</span> at <span class="set">0.2 Hz</span>, Free-Run sync (Studio) — or skip the LFO and let Uni Drift do the movement in Core.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="recipe">
      <h4>Costless filter sweep <span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem">· Core</span></h4>
      <p class="goal">A resonant-sounding sweep with zero aliasing and flat CPU.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="set">Oscillator Geometric</span>, <span class="set">Brightness 80 %</span>.</li>
        <li>Automate <span class="set">Partials</span> from <span class="set">2048</span> down to <span class="set">~8</span> over the bar — harmonics vanish from the top, smoothly, fractionally.</li>
        <li>No filter needed; add <span class="set">Formant 30 %</span> (Studio) for a peak that rides the sweep.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="deploy">
    <h2><span class="n">13</span>Beyond the DAW: engine &amp; browser</h2>
    <p class="lead">The plugin is one host for the DSP core. The same code —
      byte-for-byte the same render — runs three other ways.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Plugin</td><td>VST3 / CLAP bundle</td><td>What this handbook covers.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Native app / engine</td><td>C ABI (<code>harmonic_core.dll</code> / <code>.so</code> / <code>.dylib</code> + <code>include/harmonic_core.h</code>)</td><td><code>Voice</code> is plain-old-data (624 B, no pointers); the caller owns the memory, the crate never allocates and never panics. One voice; polyphony is the host's policy.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embedded / Eurorack</td><td><code>no_std</code> static lib, cross-compiled to Cortex-M (incl. soft-float M0), RISC-V</td><td>~14 KB. No allocator, no <code>libm</code>. Same math as the plugin, verified bit-identical.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Browser</td><td><code>wasm32</code> (~45 KB) in an AudioWorklet</td><td>An 8-voice demo with note allocation and gating in ~40 lines of JavaScript — the same job a firmware does. <a href="/demo">Play it here.</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The determinism contract — no fused multiply-add, no <code>libm</code>,
      saturating casts — means a render is IEEE-754 identical across x86-64, ARM
      and <code>wasm32</code>, at 48 <em>and</em> 96 kHz, regardless of the host's
      block size. Freeze equals real-time equals yesterday's bounce, to the bit.
      Integration details: <a href="https://github.com/Gnomir/AudioSynth">the
      repo</a>, <code>docs/08_EMBEDDED_INTEGRATION.md</code>. The engine itself is
      not tier-gated — the Core/Studio split is a plugin feature.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="wont">
    <h2><span class="n">14</span>What it won't do</h2>
    <p class="lead">Stated plainly so you don't rediscover it under deadline.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>It's not a general O(1) additive synth.</b> The closed form works for
        a spectral <em>tilt</em> plus one <em>hump</em>. An arbitrary
        per-harmonic envelope, several formants, a sharp selective notch — that's
        a filter or a different engine, not this oscillator.</li>
      <li><b>The oscillator is alias-free; the effects are not.</b> Drive, Fold,
        Grit, FM, Feedback and the soft-clip generate content above Nyquist by
        design. HQ Mode cleans it at the cost of 16 samples.</li>
      <li><b>Microtuning retunes the fundamental, not the partials.</b> Just
        intonation, historical temperaments, EDOs and Scala files all work —
        bell inharmonicity and stretched-piano tuning don't.</li>
      <li><b>2048-harmonic ceiling.</b> Below ~12 Hz the very top of the spectrum
        is lost; in practice Nyquist caps it lower anyway.</li>
      <li><b>Monotimbral.</b> One MIDI channel. Sustain, pitch bend and channel
        pressure are global; per-note brightness is the exception (addressed per
        key, so MPE works).</li>
      <li><b>Voice-steal click</b> past the polyphony limit (see <a href="#voice">§5</a>).</li>
      <li><b>No MTS-ESP client yet</b> — you can't drive it live from a host
        master-tuning source.</li>
      <li>Live-DAW testing is partial: a full pass in REAPER is done; Ableton /
        Bitwig / Logic passes are pending.</li>
      <li><b>The free Core tier holds ~17 controls at neutral</b> (see the box at
        the top). It's a licensing choice, not an engine limit.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="specs">
    <h2><span class="n">15</span>Specs &amp; verification</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Voices / channels</td><td>24, stereo out; unison 1–8 shares the pool</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Parameters</td><td>39 automatable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Latency</td><td>0 samples in Core; a constant, PDC-reported 16 in Studio (regardless of the HQ toggle — CLAP forbids changing latency outside <code>activate</code>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One clean voice</td><td>~26 M samples/s ≈ 550× realtime @ 48 kHz, flat within 1 % from 3 to 1200 harmonics</td></tr>
          <tr><td>64-voice chord</td><td>~9.4× realtime @ 48 kHz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HQ alias floor</td><td>−94.6 dBFS at +6 dB overdrive (target −75); CPU ~neutral. A dirtier patch under heavier drive measures differently — e.g. −44.9 dBFS clean / −48.5 dBFS with HQ on a Drive+Fold+Crush torture patch; see <a href="/monograph">the monograph</a> for why that's the same honest number under a harder test, not a contradiction.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Determinism</td><td>bit-identical render on x86-64 / ARM / wasm32, at 48 + 96 kHz, any block size (measured, Δ = 0.0)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tests</td><td>114 core (11 an adversarial RT-safety suite: NaN / ±∞ / parameter storms) + 34 plugin + 9 licence; run on every push by CI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validators</td><td>pluginval <code>--strictness-level 8</code> (VST3, GUI tests) · clap-validator 35 / 35</td></tr>
          <tr><td>RT-safety</td><td>audio thread never allocates, never panics (<code>panic = "abort"</code>); tested against hostile input</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Copy protection</td><td>personal watermarked Ed25519 key file, verified offline · no dongle, no activation server</td></tr>
